{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FPEy3QgntbLM4mJATLJ4B3","type":"tool_result","content":"(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\ninternal/labels:\ncatalogue.go\ncatalogue_test.go\ncolors.go\nlabels.go\nlabels_test.go\nlabelsync\nliteral_test.go\ntestdata\n\ninternal/scanner/stale:\ndecide.go\ndecide_test.go\nstale.go\nstale_test.go\ntimeline.go\ninternal/labels/catalogue.go:89:\t\t\t\tif l.Name == Pending {\ninternal/labels/catalogue.go:106:\tif sp.Intake != \"\" && !strings.EqualFold(sp.Intake, Pending) {\ninternal/labels/colors.go:30:\tPending:        {\"ededed\", \"ard: ready for triage (the lead's request)\"},\ninternal/labels/colors.go:33:\tNeedsHuman:     {\"d93f0b\", \"ard: blocked; automation stops until a human acts\"},\ninternal/labels/labels.go:43:\tPending        = \"ard:pending\"\ninternal/labels/labels.go:45:\tNeedsHuman     = \"ard:needs-human\"\ninternal/labels/labels.go:56:\treturn []string{Pending, ExecutorFailed, NeedsHuman, NeedsApproval, Hold, Approved, TestsFailed, Keep, Pause}\ninternal/labels/labels.go:60:// the single special case and returns Pending. Names are not validated here:\ninternal/labels/labels.go:65:\t\treturn Pending\ninternal/labels/labels.go:97:\tif name == \"\" || strings.EqualFold(name, Pending) {\n\ntype rawWhen struct {\n\tpaths  []string\n\ttriage []string\n}\n\n// overrideKeys lists the fields valid on every role plus those valid only\n// on one kind.\nvar commonOverrideKeys = map[string]bool{\n\t\"agent\": true, \"model\": true, \"timeout\": true, \"sidecar\": true,\n\t\"max_budget_usd\": true, \"budget_usd_per_day\": true,\n}\n\nvar kindOverrideKeys = map[Kind]map[string]bool{\n\tKindScanner:  {\"every\": true, \"max_open\": true, \"group_size\": true, \"autoclose\": true},\n\tKindTriage:   {\"after\": true, \"plan_section\": true},\n\tKindWorker:   {\"tests\": true, \"max_test_fixes\": true, \"max_review_rounds\": true, \"reviewers\": true},\n\tKindReviewer: {\"when\": true},\n}\n\n// applyOverrides merges the global role defaults and each overrides entry\n\t\t\t\tif field == \"timeout\" {\n\t\t\t\t\tov.timeout = &d\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tov.every = &d\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase \"max_open\", \"group_size\", \"max_test_fixes\", \"max_review_rounds\":\n\t\t\tvar v int\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, decodeValue(value, cfgPath, fkey, &v)...)\n\t\t\tif value.Tag == \"!!null\" {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif v < 1 {\n\t\t\t\terrs = append(errs, &Error{File: cfgPath, Key: fkey, Msg: fmt.Sprintf(\"must be at least 1 (use paused to stop a role), got %d\", v)})\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tswitch field {\n\t\t\tcase \"max_open\":\n\t\t\t\tov.maxOpen = &v\n\t\t\tcase \"group_size\":\n\t\t\t\tov.groupSize = &v\n\t\t\tcase \"max_test_fixes\":\n\t\t\t\tov.maxTestFixes = &v\n\t\t\tcase \"max_review_rounds\":\n\t\t\t\tov.maxRounds = &v\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase \"autoclose\":\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, decodeValue(value, cfgPath, fkey, &ov.autoclose)...)\n\t\tcase \"after\", \"reviewers\":\n\t\t\tlist, listErrs := decodeStringList(value, cfgPath, fkey)\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, listErrs...)\n\t\t\tif value.Tag == \"!!null\" {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif field == \"after\" {\n\t\t\t\tov.after = &list\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\tov.reviewers = &list\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase \"when\":\n\t\t\tw, whenErrs := parseWhen(value, cfgPath, fkey)\n\t\t}\n\t\trole.Every = time.Duration(*ov.every)\n\t}\n\tif ov.maxOpen != nil {\n\t\trole.MaxOpen = *ov.maxOpen\n\t}\n\tif ov.groupSize != nil {\n\t\trole.GroupSize = *ov.groupSize\n\t}\n\tif ov.autoclose != nil {\n\t\trole.Autoclose = *ov.autoclose\n\t}\n\tif ov.planSection != nil {\n\t\trole.PlanSection = *ov.planSection\n\t}\n\tif ov.after != nil {\n\tExtra      []string `yaml:\"extra,omitempty\"`\n\n\tAgent   string   `yaml:\"agent\"`\n\tModel   string   `yaml:\"model,omitempty\"`\n\tTimeout Duration `yaml:\"timeout\"`\n\tSidecar string   `yaml:\"sidecar,omitempty\"`\n\n\tEvery     Duration `yaml:\"every,omitempty\"`\n\tMaxOpen   int      `yaml:\"max_open,omitempty\"`\n\tGroupSize int      `yaml:\"group_size,omitempty\"`\n\tAutoclose bool     `yaml:\"autoclose,omitempty\"`\n\n\tAfter       []string `yaml:\"after,omitempty\"`\n\tPlanSection string   `yaml:\"plan_section,omitempty\"`\n\n\tTests           string   `yaml:\"tests,omitempty\"`\n\t\t\tExtra:           role.Extra,\n\t\t\tAgent:           role.Agent,\n\t\t\tModel:           role.Model,\n\t\t\tTimeout:         Duration(role.Timeout),\n\t\t\tSidecar:         role.Sidecar,\n\t\t\tEvery:           Duration(role.Every),\n\t\t\tMaxOpen:         role.MaxOpen,\n\t\t\tGroupSize:       role.GroupSize,\n\t\t\tAutoclose:       role.Autoclose,\n\t\t\tAfter:           role.After,\n\t\t\tPlanSection:     role.PlanSection,\n\t\t\tTests:           role.Tests,\n\t\t\tMaxTestFixes:    role.MaxTestFixes,\ndocs/proposal.md:50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\ndocs/proposal.md:63:        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\ndocs/proposal.md:81:| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\ndocs/proposal.md:87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\ndocs/proposal.md:138:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\ndocs/proposal.md:156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`.\ndocs/proposal.md:166:## Scanners\ndocs/proposal.md:198:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\ndocs/proposal.md:223:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\ndocs/proposal.md:235:**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels alone. \"Requested\" means the request label is present, \"done\" means `:done` is present. The gate waits while any triage job of the issue is running or any requested `ard:triage:<role>` lacks its `:done`; it leaves an issue alone once any `ard:worker:<role>` label exists (a worker already gated, started by a human, or in a fix round). When triage is finished it adds the lead's chosen `ard:worker:<role>`. The choice is read from the lead's close-out record in controller state, the value close-out validated, not from the committed `triage-lead.md`. It needs no pool slot and runs in the same conditions as the rest of the tick.\ndocs/proposal.md:237:Three cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate with a \"paused\" comment and `ard:needs-human`; re-admission by relabel is not offered, since a lead with `:done` is never re-claimed. A maintainer re-runs triage or adds the worker label.\ndocs/proposal.md:239:With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\ndocs/proposal.md:279:**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop at a subscription usage limit: `:running` is removed, nothing is added, the request label stays, and the issue is claimed again after the reset without using up the retry). All three commit only the redacted log. Their comment carries the reason, the last 50 log lines (cut from the redacted log, scanned again and capped at 8 KiB; if that scan flags or fails, the comment says the tail was withheld) and when the worktree and host files are kept until. `next` entries are applied only for roles that exist; a `review:<role>` the lead requests is applied when the worker opens the PR, not at triage close-out. Only changed files under `.ard/<issue#>/` that the job's allowlist permits are committed, so a newer `issue.md` on `main` is never overwritten.\ndocs/proposal.md:300:1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no label change when it is missing), and the admission: an App-authored issue passes, a human-filed one passes only when its stored body hash still matches, and a changed title or body is re-gated exactly as triage does (one comment, `ard:needs-human`).\ndocs/proposal.md:301:2. **Branch** `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` from `origin/main`. The slug is the issue title lowercased, every run of other characters turned into one `-`, at most 40 ASCII characters, `issue` when nothing is left. The title can change between rounds, so the loop looks the branch up with `git ls-remote` on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-*`: one match is reused (and fetched), none is created on the first push, and more than one (only a human can make the second) posts once and adds `ard:needs-human` without claiming. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so `worker/<role>-<issue#>-` names exactly one role and issue.\ndocs/proposal.md:304:5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. Over 1,000 changed lines in the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) also holds. The comment shows the reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\ndocs/proposal.md:307:8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are chosen by `config.SelectReviewers`: the candidates are the role's `reviewers` (a list that only narrows), or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty, and each candidate's `when:` still decides. A `when:` matches when any path changed between the merge base of the config commit and the pushed head matches a `paths` entry, or a listed triage role ran (closed `done`, or its `ard:triage:<role>:done` label; `needs-human` does not count). Paths are case-sensitive and anchored at the repo root: `web/` is a directory, anything else a doublestar glob, so `**/go.mod` matches a root `go.mod` and `go.mod` only the root one; `.ard/` and `.ard-input/` paths are never selectable. Any `review:<role>` the lead deferred or the worker's own `next` lists is added regardless of `when:`, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. Selection is monotonic: each run recomputes from the whole PR diff and close-out only adds labels, so earlier requests stay and newly matching reviewers are added. When the head or git is unavailable, path rules fail open. If no reviewer is selected the PR stays a draft (the loop logs `worker.no_reviewers`). The PR stays a draft; the merger marks it ready. A job that has pushed always gets its draft PR, even when it then ends in `ard:needs-human` or a failure, so pushed work is never left without one.\ndocs/proposal.md:312:**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. Merger conflict and rebase rounds are a follow-up that will use the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound`); until then a conflict, like a red required check, goes to `ard:needs-human`.\ndocs/proposal.md:320:1. **Poll and claim** open PRs with `ard:review:<role>`, skipping claimed, done, changes, failed and held. Reviewers get first call on free job slots, since each finished review can unblock a merge. Only PRs the App opened are reviewed: the author is the App, the base is `main` and the head is a `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` branch (human PRs are skipped and logged as `not_app_pr`). A PR is also skipped when its issue is closed, held or `ard:needs-human`, when a worker job for the issue is running, when the PR has `ard:tests:failed`, and when the issue's title or body changed after admission (the issue is re-gated exactly as the worker does, not reviewed). Reviewers of different roles run in parallel on one PR.\ndocs/proposal.md:324:5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it posts the review again with every finding in the body, and if that is also refused, as a plain PR comment. It commits `review-<role>.md` and the run's redacted output to the PR branch through the per-branch committer (no `[skip ci]`: PR-branch commits always run CI so required checks report on the head), and swaps `:running` for `:done` or `:changes`. The reviewer never edits the PR. After a close-out (and on every tick, so a restart cannot miss it) the controller marks a draft worker PR ready for review once every requested review is `:done` at the PR's current head and no `:changes`, `:running`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold` or `ard:tests:failed` label is present; it never converts a PR back to draft and never merges, which stays with the merger (Phase 2) and humans.\ndocs/proposal.md:350:- it carries none of `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:tests:failed` or `ard:executor:failed`, its issue is open and carries neither `ard:hold` nor `ard:needs-human`, and no worker or review job of the issue is running\ndocs/proposal.md:351:- the first line of the PR body is exactly `Closes #<issue>` (what the worker writes; it is what closes the issue) and the state records a worker job of the issue on this branch (its id is the `Ard-Job` trailer). Otherwise the PR gets `ard:needs-human`\ndocs/proposal.md:352:- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\ndocs/proposal.md:355:  - when the rules are readable and name required checks, each must be completed with `success`, `neutral` or `skipped` on the head (the latest run per name; a rerun in flight hides the old result). Missing or running checks wait; a red one (failure, cancelled, timed out, action required) gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment naming the checks, until #41 adds fix rounds\ndocs/proposal.md:431:- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply their labels on the author's behalf, so an outsider can file an issue that already carries the intake label. That is why, in label mode, the controller honours the label only if the latest `labeled` event came from an actor with triage or higher permission, or from the App; otherwise it adds `ard:needs-human`. The intake label must start with `ard:`, and a label ard itself applies (`ard:needs-human`, a stage label and so on) cannot be the intake label; `ard config check` and `ard labels sync` reject it, and `ard serve` never admits through it. In both modes the issue is re-gated if its body is edited afterwards. Note that `all` mode lets any issue author start a job, so use it only on repos where that is acceptable. **The one exception to \"the issue body is human-owned untrusted input\" is ard's cost block.** ard owns only the text between its `ard:cost` markers. The admission hash (`agentjob.BodyHash`) and the `issue.md` snapshot given to agents both use the body with that block stripped (`costblock.Strip`), so whatever the hash ignores is exactly what no agent sees: text hidden from the re-gate can never reach an agent. Strip removes only the last begin-to-end block, and only when its contents parse as exactly the table ard renders (fixed header, rows of validated cells, a Total row, at most 5,000 bytes; LF or CRLF); a fake block, markers around prose, or an edited table is not stripped, so it stays visible to agents and counts in the hash and an edit re-gates as usual. A body with no valid block hashes exactly as before, so stored admissions stay valid. ard never overwrites or drops such human text: it appends its own block after it. The block's header says so (\"do not edit: an edited block is kept as text, shown to agents and re-gates the issue\"). ard's body writes bump `updated_at`.\ndocs/proposal.md:439:- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n.ard/scanners:\ncve\n\n.ard/scanners/cve:\nmerge.jq\nscan\n\ninternal/scanner/testdata:\nbodies","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"16a43ddf-b1f2-4a1a-bd50-cc2e68a999c2","uuid":"ed618391-da51-48c2-8eb3-a725ff8958fd","timestamp":"2026-11-23T17:10:46.175Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\n(eval):1: no matches found: --include=*.go\ninternal/labels:\ncatalogue.go\ncatalogue_test.go\ncolors.go\nlabels.go\nlabels_test.go\nlabelsync\nliteral_test.go\ntestdata\n\ninternal/scanner/stale:\ndecide.go\ndecide_test.go\nstale.go\nstale_test.go\ntimeline.go\ninternal/labels/catalogue.go:89:\t\t\t\tif l.Name == Pending {\ninternal/labels/catalogue.go:106:\tif sp.Intake != \"\" && !strings.EqualFold(sp.Intake, Pending) {\ninternal/labels/colors.go:30:\tPending:        {\"ededed\", \"ard: ready for triage (the lead's request)\"},\ninternal/labels/colors.go:33:\tNeedsHuman:     {\"d93f0b\", \"ard: blocked; automation stops until a human acts\"},\ninternal/labels/labels.go:43:\tPending        = \"ard:pending\"\ninternal/labels/labels.go:45:\tNeedsHuman     = \"ard:needs-human\"\ninternal/labels/labels.go:56:\treturn []string{Pending, ExecutorFailed, NeedsHuman, NeedsApproval, Hold, Approved, TestsFailed, Keep, Pause}\ninternal/labels/labels.go:60:// the single special case and returns Pending. Names are not validated here:\ninternal/labels/labels.go:65:\t\treturn Pending\ninternal/labels/labels.go:97:\tif name == \"\" || strings.EqualFold(name, Pending) {\n\ntype rawWhen struct {\n\tpaths  []string\n\ttriage []string\n}\n\n// overrideKeys lists the fields valid on every role plus those valid only\n// on one kind.\nvar commonOverrideKeys = map[string]bool{\n\t\"agent\": true, \"model\": true, \"timeout\": true, \"sidecar\": true,\n\t\"max_budget_usd\": true, \"budget_usd_per_day\": true,\n}\n\nvar kindOverrideKeys = map[Kind]map[string]bool{\n\tKindScanner:  {\"every\": true, \"max_open\": true, \"group_size\": true, \"autoclose\": true},\n\tKindTriage:   {\"after\": true, \"plan_section\": true},\n\tKindWorker:   {\"tests\": true, \"max_test_fixes\": true, \"max_review_rounds\": true, \"reviewers\": true},\n\tKindReviewer: {\"when\": true},\n}\n\n// applyOverrides merges the global role defaults and each overrides entry\n\t\t\t\tif field == \"timeout\" {\n\t\t\t\t\tov.timeout = &d\n\t\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\t\tov.every = &d\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase \"max_open\", \"group_size\", \"max_test_fixes\", \"max_review_rounds\":\n\t\t\tvar v int\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, decodeValue(value, cfgPath, fkey, &v)...)\n\t\t\tif value.Tag == \"!!null\" {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif v < 1 {\n\t\t\t\terrs = append(errs, &Error{File: cfgPath, Key: fkey, Msg: fmt.Sprintf(\"must be at least 1 (use paused to stop a role), got %d\", v)})\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tswitch field {\n\t\t\tcase \"max_open\":\n\t\t\t\tov.maxOpen = &v\n\t\t\tcase \"group_size\":\n\t\t\t\tov.groupSize = &v\n\t\t\tcase \"max_test_fixes\":\n\t\t\t\tov.maxTestFixes = &v\n\t\t\tcase \"max_review_rounds\":\n\t\t\t\tov.maxRounds = &v\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase \"autoclose\":\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, decodeValue(value, cfgPath, fkey, &ov.autoclose)...)\n\t\tcase \"after\", \"reviewers\":\n\t\t\tlist, listErrs := decodeStringList(value, cfgPath, fkey)\n\t\t\terrs = append(errs, listErrs...)\n\t\t\tif value.Tag == \"!!null\" {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif field == \"after\" {\n\t\t\t\tov.after = &list\n\t\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t\tov.reviewers = &list\n\t\t\t}\n\t\tcase \"when\":\n\t\t\tw, whenErrs := parseWhen(value, cfgPath, fkey)\n\t\t}\n\t\trole.Every = time.Duration(*ov.every)\n\t}\n\tif ov.maxOpen != nil {\n\t\trole.MaxOpen = *ov.maxOpen\n\t}\n\tif ov.groupSize != nil {\n\t\trole.GroupSize = *ov.groupSize\n\t}\n\tif ov.autoclose != nil {\n\t\trole.Autoclose = *ov.autoclose\n\t}\n\tif ov.planSection != nil {\n\t\trole.PlanSection = *ov.planSection\n\t}\n\tif ov.after != nil {\n\tExtra      []string `yaml:\"extra,omitempty\"`\n\n\tAgent   string   `yaml:\"agent\"`\n\tModel   string   `yaml:\"model,omitempty\"`\n\tTimeout Duration `yaml:\"timeout\"`\n\tSidecar string   `yaml:\"sidecar,omitempty\"`\n\n\tEvery     Duration `yaml:\"every,omitempty\"`\n\tMaxOpen   int      `yaml:\"max_open,omitempty\"`\n\tGroupSize int      `yaml:\"group_size,omitempty\"`\n\tAutoclose bool     `yaml:\"autoclose,omitempty\"`\n\n\tAfter       []string `yaml:\"after,omitempty\"`\n\tPlanSection string   `yaml:\"plan_section,omitempty\"`\n\n\tTests           string   `yaml:\"tests,omitempty\"`\n\t\t\tExtra:           role.Extra,\n\t\t\tAgent:           role.Agent,\n\t\t\tModel:           role.Model,\n\t\t\tTimeout:         Duration(role.Timeout),\n\t\t\tSidecar:         role.Sidecar,\n\t\t\tEvery:           Duration(role.Every),\n\t\t\tMaxOpen:         role.MaxOpen,\n\t\t\tGroupSize:       role.GroupSize,\n\t\t\tAutoclose:       role.Autoclose,\n\t\t\tAfter:           role.After,\n\t\t\tPlanSection:     role.PlanSection,\n\t\t\tTests:           role.Tests,\n\t\t\tMaxTestFixes:    role.MaxTestFixes,\ndocs/proposal.md:50:Every label starts with `ard:`, and every stage uses one grammar: `ard:<stage>:<role>` requests work, `:running` marks a claim, `:done` marks completion. A loop picks up an issue that has the request label and none of `:running`, `:done`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:hold`.\ndocs/proposal.md:63:        ard:hold, ard:needs-human and ard:pause\ndocs/proposal.md:81:| `ard:needs-human` | Controller, from the agent's result | Agent reported it is blocked; automation stops |\ndocs/proposal.md:87:**One query per role per poll.** For example: `is:open label:\"ard:triage:architect\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:running\" -label:\"ard:triage:architect:done\" -label:ard:executor:failed -label:ard:needs-human -label:ard:hold`. Six triage roles every 15 minutes, plus four reviewers, a worker and the merger every 5, is about 100 searches an hour, well under GitHub's limits.\ndocs/proposal.md:138:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human (both of the last two add ard:needs-human)\ndocs/proposal.md:156:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`.\ndocs/proposal.md:166:## Scanners\ndocs/proposal.md:198:**The stale closer is a built-in scanner.** It has no role directory and needs no job slot, executor or worktree; its settings live in the top-level `stale:` block of `config.yaml`. It is a plain controller loop that runs every 24 hours and is skipped while paused. It closes scanner issues still at `ard:pending` with no activity for `stale.inactive_for` (default 30 days), as not planned, with a comment. Activity means a timeline event by anyone other than the ard App. It never closes an issue that has a `:running` label, `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, a linked open PR, or a human author (any author other than the App), and it closes nothing when the client is not authenticated as an App. It only comments and closes: suppression happens on the owning scanner's next reconcile, which suppresses every not-planned close for `stale.suppress_for`. A reopened issue gets a fresh comment when it goes stale again.\ndocs/proposal.md:223:1. Query for the role's request label (the lead's is `ard:pending`), excluding claimed, done, failed, blocked and held issues. Which human-filed issues receive `ard:pending` is the `intake` setting (decided, #2): in `all` mode every human-filed issue enters with no label needed; in `label` mode only issues carrying the configured intake label do. `intake.Admit` decides, as a pure function of the issue, its timeline and the labeler's permission, and it fails closed. Issues authored by the App (scanner-filed) always pass. In `all` mode only issues opened after `all` mode was first enabled enter without a label; older issues still need the label. In `label` mode only the most recent `labeled` event for the label counts: it must come from the App or from a collaborator with role triage, write, maintain or admin (matched case-insensitively), and an earlier authorised event does not rescue a later unauthorised one. When the label is present but not honoured, the controller adds `ard:needs-human` with a short comment asking a maintainer to re-apply the label. The loop records each admission in the state `admissions` table (`labeled_by`, `labeled_at`, `body_hash`); if the title or body is edited after admission, the issue is re-gated with `ard:needs-human` and a comment until a maintainer re-applies the intake label. App-authored issues are exempt from the hash check. The loop never waits for a job: each claimed job is handed to a watcher goroutine that closes out through the shared Finisher, and a restart leaves running jobs to pool adoption and the Reaper. The re-gate hash covers the title and the body without ard's own cost block (see Guardrails, \"Intake is configurable\"), so ard's table writes never re-gate an issue. The first time ard writes the block, the stored hash stays valid; a maintainer relabel that falls between a human edit and ard's next table write may need repeating, because an ard body write bumps the issue's `updated_at`, which fails safe.\ndocs/proposal.md:235:**A deterministic gate starts the worker.** Once per triage tick, after the intake sweep, the controller searches for open issues that carry `ard:triage:lead:done` and none of `ard:pending`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` or any configured worker's request label (the exclusion keeps gated issues from filling the 100-hit window). It re-reads each hit and decides from labels alone. \"Requested\" means the request label is present, \"done\" means `:done` is present. The gate waits while any triage job of the issue is running or any requested `ard:triage:<role>` lacks its `:done`; it leaves an issue alone once any `ard:worker:<role>` label exists (a worker already gated, started by a human, or in a fix round). When triage is finished it adds the lead's chosen `ard:worker:<role>`. The choice is read from the lead's close-out record in controller state, the value close-out validated, not from the committed `triage-lead.md`. It needs no pool slot and runs in the same conditions as the rest of the tick.\ndocs/proposal.md:237:Three cases go to a human, each with one comment and `ard:needs-human`, and nothing is ever closed automatically: the lead finished `done` but named no valid worker (or its close-out record is missing), the chosen worker role no longer exists, or an `ard:triage:<role>` is requested for a role that does not exist. A title or body edit after admission, on a human-filed issue, also stops the gate with a \"paused\" comment and `ard:needs-human`; re-admission by relabel is not offered, since a lead with `:done` is never re-claimed. A maintainer re-runs triage or adds the worker label.\ndocs/proposal.md:239:With `require_approval: true` (the Phase 1 default) the gate posts one \"ready for approval\" comment per lead job and waits. `ard:approved` is honoured only when its latest `labeled` event (reset by an `unlabeled`) is by a collaborator with triage permission or higher, is not the App, and comes after the last triage `:done` label of the issue. An approval given earlier is left in place and ignored: the gate waits for a fresh one and does not escalate. An approval from anyone else adds `ard:needs-human` with a comment that does not name the actor. A human who adds `ard:worker:<role>` directly bypasses the gate and has approved by doing so. The gate does not enforce that `triage:engineer` ran: the lead routes and the gate applies its routing. In `--dry-run` the gate logs `triage.would_gate` and writes nothing; otherwise each action records a `gate` event.\ndocs/proposal.md:279:**Close-out is resumable.** The controller (`internal/closeout`) decides the outcome from the role file (only one this job wrote), stages the redacted files, then commits, requests the `next` labels, posts one summary comment (for a review with a verdict, one PR review instead; either is tagged `<!-- ard:closeout job=<id> -->` so a replay never posts twice) and finally swaps `:running` for `:done`, `:changes`, `ard:needs-human` or `ard:executor:failed`. It records its phase in local state after each step. `serve` resumes unfinished close-outs at startup, and the Reaper resumes, rather than fails, a job whose close-out has started. Three more outcomes exist for a job that ended without a usable result: `failed` (sets `ard:executor:failed`), `retry` (a named transient cause: no `ard:executor:failed`, the request label kept, the next claim is the automatic retry) and `usage-limit` (a stop at a subscription usage limit: `:running` is removed, nothing is added, the request label stays, and the issue is claimed again after the reset without using up the retry). All three commit only the redacted log. Their comment carries the reason, the last 50 log lines (cut from the redacted log, scanned again and capped at 8 KiB; if that scan flags or fails, the comment says the tail was withheld) and when the worktree and host files are kept until. `next` entries are applied only for roles that exist; a `review:<role>` the lead requests is applied when the worker opens the PR, not at triage close-out. Only changed files under `.ard/<issue#>/` that the job's allowlist permits are committed, so a newer `issue.md` on `main` is never overwritten.\ndocs/proposal.md:300:1. **Poll and claim** `ard:worker:<role>` (added by the triage fan-in gate; the worker never adds or removes it) like triage, also skipping `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold`, `ard:executor:failed` and an issue that has a running triage job or any running worker job. Workers rank second for job slots, after reviewers. Before claiming, the loop re-checks `ard:approved` when `require_approval` is on (no label change when it is missing), and the admission: an App-authored issue passes, a human-filed one passes only when its stored body hash still matches, and a changed title or body is re-gated exactly as triage does (one comment, `ard:needs-human`).\ndocs/proposal.md:301:2. **Branch** `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` from `origin/main`. The slug is the issue title lowercased, every run of other characters turned into one `-`, at most 40 ASCII characters, `issue` when nothing is left. The title can change between rounds, so the loop looks the branch up with `git ls-remote` on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-*`: one match is reused (and fetched), none is created on the first push, and more than one (only a human can make the second) posts once and adds `ard:needs-human` without claiming. Worker role names may not contain an all-digit segment (`eng-12`), so `worker/<role>-<issue#>-` names exactly one role and issue.\ndocs/proposal.md:304:5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names the variable, never the value), and the built-in pattern scanner runs over it with only the findings on lines this job added kept, so a worker that edits a file that already holds a credential-shaped fixture is not held for lines it did not write. The comment names file, line and rule (`internal/foo_test.go:12: secret scan rule aws-access-key`), never the text. A scanner error fails closed. Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`); a literal is held, and there is no in-band escape. Over 1,000 changed lines in the whole PR (the diff against the merge base with `main`) also holds. The comment shows the reason as `diff guard: <entry>; <entry>; <entry>; and N more`.\ndocs/proposal.md:307:8. **Open or update a draft PR** with ard's own `Closes #<issue>`, the agent summary quoted and neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`, then marker and mention defusing), the test result and links to `worker-<role>.md`, the logs and the job id. Reviewers are chosen by `config.SelectReviewers`: the candidates are the role's `reviewers` (a list that only narrows), or every reviewer role when it is unset or empty, and each candidate's `when:` still decides. A `when:` matches when any path changed between the merge base of the config commit and the pushed head matches a `paths` entry, or a listed triage role ran (closed `done`, or its `ard:triage:<role>:done` label; `needs-human` does not count). Paths are case-sensitive and anchored at the repo root: `web/` is a directory, anything else a doublestar glob, so `**/go.mod` matches a root `go.mod` and `go.mod` only the root one; `.ard/` and `.ard-input/` paths are never selectable. Any `review:<role>` the lead deferred or the worker's own `next` lists is added regardless of `when:`, limited to roles that exist, and requested only when the tests did not fail. Selection is monotonic: each run recomputes from the whole PR diff and close-out only adds labels, so earlier requests stay and newly matching reviewers are added. When the head or git is unavailable, path rules fail open. If no reviewer is selected the PR stays a draft (the loop logs `worker.no_reviewers`). The PR stays a draft; the merger marks it ready. A job that has pushed always gets its draft PR, even when it then ends in `ard:needs-human` or a failure, so pushed work is never left without one.\ndocs/proposal.md:312:**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. Merger conflict and rebase rounds are a follow-up that will use the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound`); until then a conflict, like a red required check, goes to `ard:needs-human`.\ndocs/proposal.md:320:1. **Poll and claim** open PRs with `ard:review:<role>`, skipping claimed, done, changes, failed and held. Reviewers get first call on free job slots, since each finished review can unblock a merge. Only PRs the App opened are reviewed: the author is the App, the base is `main` and the head is a `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>` branch (human PRs are skipped and logged as `not_app_pr`). A PR is also skipped when its issue is closed, held or `ard:needs-human`, when a worker job for the issue is running, when the PR has `ard:tests:failed`, and when the issue's title or body changed after admission (the issue is re-gated exactly as the worker does, not reviewed). Reviewers of different roles run in parallel on one PR.\ndocs/proposal.md:324:5. **Close-out.** The controller posts one PR review at the pinned commit (event `COMMENT`): findings on lines the PR added are inline comments, every other finding is listed in the review body. If GitHub answers 422 (a line or the commit is not in the PR), it posts the review again with every finding in the body, and if that is also refused, as a plain PR comment. It commits `review-<role>.md` and the run's redacted output to the PR branch through the per-branch committer (no `[skip ci]`: PR-branch commits always run CI so required checks report on the head), and swaps `:running` for `:done` or `:changes`. The reviewer never edits the PR. After a close-out (and on every tick, so a restart cannot miss it) the controller marks a draft worker PR ready for review once every requested review is `:done` at the PR's current head and no `:changes`, `:running`, `ard:executor:failed`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:hold` or `ard:tests:failed` label is present; it never converts a PR back to draft and never merges, which stays with the merger (Phase 2) and humans.\ndocs/proposal.md:350:- it carries none of `ard:hold`, `ard:needs-human`, `ard:tests:failed` or `ard:executor:failed`, its issue is open and carries neither `ard:hold` nor `ard:needs-human`, and no worker or review job of the issue is running\ndocs/proposal.md:351:- the first line of the PR body is exactly `Closes #<issue>` (what the worker writes; it is what closes the issue) and the state records a worker job of the issue on this branch (its id is the `Ard-Job` trailer). Otherwise the PR gets `ard:needs-human`\ndocs/proposal.md:352:- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\ndocs/proposal.md:355:  - when the rules are readable and name required checks, each must be completed with `success`, `neutral` or `skipped` on the head (the latest run per name; a rerun in flight hides the old result). Missing or running checks wait; a red one (failure, cancelled, timed out, action required) gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment naming the checks, until #41 adds fix rounds\ndocs/proposal.md:431:- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply their labels on the author's behalf, so an outsider can file an issue that already carries the intake label. That is why, in label mode, the controller honours the label only if the latest `labeled` event came from an actor with triage or higher permission, or from the App; otherwise it adds `ard:needs-human`. The intake label must start with `ard:`, and a label ard itself applies (`ard:needs-human`, a stage label and so on) cannot be the intake label; `ard config check` and `ard labels sync` reject it, and `ard serve` never admits through it. In both modes the issue is re-gated if its body is edited afterwards. Note that `all` mode lets any issue author start a job, so use it only on repos where that is acceptable. **The one exception to \"the issue body is human-owned untrusted input\" is ard's cost block.** ard owns only the text between its `ard:cost` markers. The admission hash (`agentjob.BodyHash`) and the `issue.md` snapshot given to agents both use the body with that block stripped (`costblock.Strip`), so whatever the hash ignores is exactly what no agent sees: text hidden from the re-gate can never reach an agent. Strip removes only the last begin-to-end block, and only when its contents parse as exactly the table ard renders (fixed header, rows of validated cells, a Total row, at most 5,000 bytes; LF or CRLF); a fake block, markers around prose, or an edited table is not stripped, so it stays visible to agents and counts in the hash and an edit re-gates as usual. A body with no valid block hashes exactly as before, so stored admissions stay valid. ard never overwrites or drops such human text: it appends its own block after it. The block's header says so (\"do not edit: an edited block is kept as text, shown to agents and re-gates the issue\"). ard's body writes bump `updated_at`.\ndocs/proposal.md:439:- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n.ard/scanners:\ncve\n\n.ard/scanners/cve:\nmerge.jq\nscan\n\ninternal/scanner/testdata:\nbodies","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FPEy3QgntbLM4mJATLJ4B3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
