{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLENuk171mmQNpgLHEFV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ANfDLDr4SAgztqaYMnpZQs","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; cat > .ard/215/plan.md <<'EOF'\n# Plan for #215\n\n## Implementation\n\n### Steps\n\n1. `internal/labels/labels.go` (read first; constants `Pending`, `NeedsHuman` exist): no change unless a helper is wanted. Add in `internal/config/types.go` (new) `type Route string` with consts `RoutePending = \"pending\"`, `RouteNone = \"none\"`, `RouteNeedsHuman = \"needs-human\"`, and a field `Route Route` on `Role` under \"Scanner only\". Reason: one typed key every consumer reads.\n2. `internal/config/load.go`: add `\"route\": true` to `kindOverrideKeys[KindScanner]`; add `route *Route`-style field (`*string`) to the override struct (near `autoclose`, line ~55); add a `case \"route\":` in the override decode switch (near line 261) that decodes a string, rejects any value outside the three constants with `&Error{File: cfgPath, Key: fkey, Msg: \"must be pending, none or needs-human, got ...\"}`, and applies it in the apply block (near line 374). In `applyRoleDefaults` (`KindScanner` case, line ~181) set `role.Route = RoutePending` when `role.ScanPath != \"\"`, else `RouteNone`. Add a post-resolution check function `validateRoute(role Role) *Error` that returns an error for `Route == RoutePending && ScanPath == \"\"` (LLM scanner); called after overrides so it is the hook for #204 (today LLM scanners are skipped by the pipeline, so only the check exists). `route` on a triage, worker or reviewer role is already rejected as \"unknown override key\" by the `allowed` test at line ~204; keep that and confirm `wrongKindMsg` gives a useful message (extend it if it lists kinds per key).\n3. `internal/config/render.go`: add `Route string \\`yaml:\"route,omitempty\"\\`` to the rendered role struct (line ~89) and `Route: string(role.Route)` in the builder (line ~158), so `ard config` output and any golden tests show it. Update golden files under `internal/config/testdata` if they list scanner fields.\n4. `internal/scanner/guard.go` (new): \n   - `type Guard struct { Secrets *redact.Secrets; Scanner redact.Scanner }` and `func (g Guard) Check(ctx context.Context, fs []Finding) *GuardHit`; `type GuardHit struct { Index int; Rule, Field string; Err error }`, `GuardHit.Error()` never contains text.\n   - For every finding index i and each decoded field (`title`, `body`, `files[j]` reported as `files`, `fingerprint`, `group`) run, in order: the known-value check (`Secrets.Names([]byte(v))` non-empty, rule `known-value`, never the variable's value), then `Scanner.Scan(ctx, strings.NewReader(v))` (rule = `redact.Finding.Rule`). Scanning field by field gives the field name; the scan runs in a goroutine-safe wrapper like `redact.Redactor.scan` (copy the ctx-cancel pattern) so a cancelled context fails closed. Any scanner error returns a `GuardHit` with `Rule: \"scan-error\"` and the field.\n   - `func DefaultGuard() Guard` returns `redact.Builtin()` plus `redact.FromEnviron(os.Environ())`, used when `Pipeline.Guard` is unset, so there is no way to turn the guard off.\n5. `internal/scanner/pipeline.go`:\n   - Add `Guard func() Guard` to `Pipeline` (nil means `DefaultGuard()`), documented as \"no opt-out; nil is not disabled\".\n   - Right after the paused check and `res.Found = len(sr.Findings)` (line ~236) and before `Diff`/`ex.edits`, run `p.guard().Check`. On a hit: `log.Warn(\"scanner.guard_flagged\", \"finding\", hit.Index, \"rule\", hit.Rule, \"field\", hit.Field)` (only these keys; never title, fingerprint or the error text), `record(OutcomeScanFailed)`, return `res, errors.Join(errs...)`. Nothing after this point has run, so no create, join, edit, tick, close or row write happens. This also runs under dry-run (no `dry` condition).\n   - `create`: labels become `[]string{labels.Scanner(name), <route label>}` via a new `func routeLabels(r config.Role) []string`: `pending` -> `labels.Pending`; `none` -> none; `needs-human` -> `labels.NeedsHuman`.\n   - `joinableIssue(lbls, scanner, p, group)`: add a `route config.Route` parameter; the required label is `labels.Pending` / none / `labels.NeedsHuman`; the \"no other ard: label\" loop allows the scanner label and the route label. Update the two callers (`joinable`, `join`).\n   - `fetch` synthetic branch (line ~287): labels = `append([]string{labels.Scanner(name)}, routeLabelOf...)` using `routeLabels(e.role)` so dry-run joins match.\n   - `openIssues` stays keyed on the scanner label, so counting is unchanged for all routes.\n6. Stale closer: `internal/scanner/stale/decide.go` and `stale.go` already require `ard:pending` and exclude `ard:needs-human`, so `needs-human` and `none` issues are never closed. No code change; lock this in with tests (below).\n7. `internal/servecmd/scanners.go`: set `Guard` on the `Pipeline` literal from `w.Secrets` style wiring: `Guard: func() scanner.Guard { ... }` building `redact.FromEnviron(os.Environ())` and, when `w.gitleaksOptions()` (`internal/servecmd/reaping.go:39`) reports enabled, `redact.Multi(redact.Builtin(), redact.Gitleaks(opts))`. Read per run so a config change applies at once, as `closeout.JobRedactor` does.\n8. `docs/proposal.md`: in \"## Scanners\" (line ~166) add a paragraph for `route:` (three values, defaults, LLM scanners may not use `pending`, a human adds `ard:pending` to send one to triage, `needs-human` is never triaged and the stale closer ignores it) and for the pre-filing guard (fields scanned, builtin + optional gitleaks + known values, fail closed, `scan_failed`, logs rule and field only, runs in dry-run). Change the label-table row (line 81) to: `ard:needs-human` | \"Controller, from an agent result, or a scanner at filing\" | ...; keep the meaning column. Mention in the stale-closer paragraph (line 198) that `route: needs-human` issues lack `ard:pending` and so are never candidates.\n\n### Test plan\n\nCommand: `go test ./internal/config/... ./internal/scanner/... ./internal/servecmd/... ./internal/roles/...`, then `make lint` and `make test`. Build test strings for secrets at run time (`\"AK\" + \"IA\" + ...`), never as literals (the worker diff guard holds literal credentials).\n\n- `internal/config/load_test.go`: `TestRouteValid` (each of three values loads); `TestRouteBadValue` (`route: sometimes` is an error naming the key); `TestRouteOnNonScanner` (`route: none` on a triage/worker/reviewer role is an error); `TestRouteDefaults` (executable scanner -> `pending`, LLM scanner -> `none`); `TestRoutePendingOnLLMScanner` (LLM scanner with `route: pending` errors). `render_test.go`: the route shows in the rendered config.\n- `internal/scanner/guard_test.go`: table over fields `title`, `body`, `files`, `fingerprint`, `group` each holding a built-at-runtime secret: hit with the right index, rule and field; clean finding: no hit; known-value hit (secrets built with `Secrets.Add`) -> rule `known-value`, error text lacks the value; failing scanner -> `scan-error`; cancelled context -> hit; the hit's `Error()` and log never contain the text.\n- `internal/scanner/pipeline_test.go` (use `harness_test.go`): `TestGuardBlocksFiling` for each field: outcome `OutcomeScanFailed`, zero `CreateIssue`/`UpdateIssueBody`/`CreateComment`/`UpdateIssue` calls, store rows unchanged, log has `scanner.guard_flagged` with `finding`, `rule`, `field` only; `TestGuardRunsInDryRun`; `TestGuardBlocksEditsAndCloses` (a finding that would tick or autoclose an existing issue is not applied when another finding is flagged); `TestGuardGitleaksMissing` (scanner stub that errors) fails closed; `TestGuardDefaultWhenNil` (nil `Guard` still scans).\n- Route tests: `TestRouteNeedsHumanCreate` (labels are scanner + `ard:needs-human`, no `ard:pending`); `TestRouteNoneCreate` (scanner label only); `TestRoutePendingUnchanged`; `TestJoinUnderNeedsHuman` / `TestJoinUnderNone` (join accepts the route label, refuses an issue with an extra ard: label or the wrong route); `TestDryRunSyntheticJoinNeedsHuman`; `TestAutocloseNeedsHuman` (all lines checked -> issue closed); `TestJoinableIssue` table updated.\n- `internal/scanner/stale/decide_test.go`: `TestStaleSkipsNeedsHumanRouteIssue` (scanner label + `ard:needs-human`, no pending -> not a candidate) and the same for a scanner-label-only issue.\n- `internal/roles/cve_test.go` (or new test): every `internal/roles/testdata/cve/*/want.jsonl` decodes with `DecodeFindings` and passes `DefaultGuard().Check` with no hit. (Needs the `roles` package to import `scanner`; if that is an import cycle, put the test in `internal/scanner` reading `../roles/testdata/cve`.)\n- Edge cases: empty `files`, empty `group`, finding with only a title, very long body (scanner window), unicode.\n\n### Risk\n\nMedium. It changes the shared pipeline used by every scanner (create labels, join check, new early return), so a mistake affects `cve` filing. No migration and no new run outcome, no change to protected paths (`.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/scanners/` untouched; docs and Go only; `docs/proposal.md` is not protected). `joinableIssue` and `Role` are internal interfaces. The guard handles secret material: it must never log or return the matched text. The gitleaks pass runs a subprocess per field if scanned naively; mitigate by scanning one joined, newline-separated buffer per finding and mapping line numbers back to fields, or by accepting per-field runs (slow) — decide in the worker; prefer the single-buffer approach.\n\n### Size\n\nL (roughly 500 to 800 changed lines including tests and docs). Files: `internal/config/types.go`, `load.go`, `render.go`, `load_test.go`, `render_test.go`; `internal/scanner/guard.go` (new), `guard_test.go` (new), `pipeline.go`, `pipeline_test.go`, `harness_test.go`; `internal/scanner/stale/decide_test.go`; `internal/servecmd/scanners.go`; a cve fixture test in `internal/roles` or `internal/scanner`; `docs/proposal.md`.\nEOF\ncat > .ard/215/triage-engineer.md <<'EOF'\n---\nrole: engineer\nstatus: done\nattempt: 1\n---\nAdds a scanner `route` key (config schema, validation, and label handling in the pipeline's create, join and dry-run paths) and a fail-closed pre-filing secret guard that runs on every scanner, then updates the proposal. The plan has eight steps with tests for each field, the routes, the stale closer and the cve fixtures. Size L, risk medium because the shared pipeline serves every scanner.\n\n## Open issues\n- Reconcile and the dry-run ledger adoption run before the scan, so they can still refresh local finding rows before the guard runs. The plan puts the guard before any diff, edit, join, create, tick or close, which is the earliest point the findings exist; a maintainer should confirm that is what \"no finding row changes\" means.\n- The stale closer already ignores issues without `ard:pending` and issues with `ard:needs-human`, so no code change is planned there, only tests.\n- Running gitleaks once per field would be slow; the worker is told to prefer one buffer per finding.\nEOF"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1685,"cache_read_input_tokens":49202,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1685},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"16a43ddf-b1f2-4a1a-bd50-cc2e68a999c2","uuid":"20f41a0c-6059-400b-9794-0fd9d1667e31","timestamp":"2026-11-23T17:11:37.025Z","request_id":"req_011ChLENuaLLTy7zzwdTtTY7"}
